**2.3 Tenant Isolation Enforcement**

Isolation is enforced at three independent layers. All three must pass
for data to be returned:

  -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  **Layer**        **Mechanism**                                **What It Prevents**
  ---------------- -------------------------------------------- ---------------------------
  Database (RLS)   PostgreSQL Row-Level Security policies       A compromised API layer
                   applied to every table. Policy: WHERE        still cannot return
                   tenant_id =                                  cross-tenant data --- the
                   current_setting(\'app.current_tenant_id\')   database rejects the query

  API /            Every authenticated request extracts         Application bugs or missing
  Application      tenant_id from the JWT token. All            WHERE clauses cannot
                   service-layer queries include tenant_id as a accidentally return
                   mandatory filter parameter                   cross-tenant rows

  Authentication   JWT tokens include tenant_id claim. Users    A user from Tenant A cannot
                   belong to exactly one tenant. Token          authenticate as a user in
                   validation rejects requests where the        Tenant B, even with a valid
                   tenant_id claim does not match the route     token
                   tenant context                               
  -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  **Security Principle: Defense in depth --- tenant isolation is NOT
  solely dependent on application code correctness. The database enforces
  it independently. Even if a developer forgets to add a WHERE tenant_id
  clause to a query, the RLS policy at the database layer will reject the
  query or return zero rows.**

  -----------------------------------------------------------------------

**3. Database Architecture**

**3.1 Database Selection**

Recommended database: PostgreSQL (v15+). Rationale:

-   Native Row-Level Security (RLS) --- the core enforcement mechanism
    for tenant isolation

-   JSONB columns for flexible per-tenant settings and trade-specific
    data fields without schema bloat

-   Mature, battle-tested at SaaS scale --- used by major multi-tenant
    platforms

-   Strong support on all major cloud providers (AWS RDS/Aurora, GCP
    Cloud SQL, DigitalOcean Managed PostgreSQL)

-   UUID primary keys --- prevents ID enumeration attacks across tenants

**3.2 Row-Level Security Implementation**

**RLS Policy Pattern (applied to every tenant-scoped table)**

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+
| \-- Enable RLS on every tenant-scoped table                           |
|                                                                       |
| ALTER TABLE projects ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;                       |
|                                                                       |
| \-- Policy: users can only SELECT rows belonging to their tenant      |
|                                                                       |
| CREATE POLICY tenant_isolation_select ON projects                     |
|                                                                       |
| FOR SELECT                                                            |
|                                                                       |
| USING (tenant_id = current_setting(\'app.current_tenant_id\')::UUID); |
|                                                                       |
| \-- Policy: users can only INSERT rows for their tenant               |
|                                                                       |
| CREATE POLICY tenant_isolation_insert ON projects                     |
|                                                                       |
| FOR INSERT                                                            |
|                                                                       |
| WITH CHECK (tenant_id =                                               |
| current_setting(\'app.current_tenant_id\')::UUID);                    |
|                                                                       |
| \-- Policy: users can only UPDATE rows belonging to their tenant      |
|                                                                       |
| CREATE POLICY tenant_isolation_update ON projects                     |
|                                                                       |
| FOR UPDATE                                                            |
|                                                                       |
| USING (tenant_id = current_setting(\'app.current_tenant_id\')::UUID); |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+

**Session Configuration (set at connection time)**

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+
| \-- Set tenant context at the start of every database                 |
| session/transaction                                                   |
|                                                                       |
| \-- Called by the API layer after JWT validation                      |
|                                                                       |
| SET LOCAL app.current_tenant_id = \'t_001\'; \-- extracted from       |
| validated JWT                                                         |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+

**3.3 Primary Key Strategy**

All primary keys use UUID v4 (randomly generated). This is critical for
multi-tenant security: prevents ID enumeration, globally unique, no
information leakage.

**3.4 Database Indexing Strategy**

Every tenant-scoped table requires compound indexes that include
tenant_id as the leading column:

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+
| CREATE INDEX idx_projects_tenant ON projects (tenant_id);             |
|                                                                       |
| CREATE INDEX idx_projects_tenant_status ON projects (tenant_id,       |
| status);                                                              |
|                                                                       |
| CREATE INDEX idx_tasks_tenant_project ON tasks (tenant_id,            |
| project_id);                                                          |
|                                                                       |
| CREATE INDEX idx_documents_tenant_proj ON documents (tenant_id,       |
| project_id);                                                          |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+

Without tenant_id as the leading index column, queries that filter by
tenant_id will perform full table scans as the platform grows.

**3.5 Database Scaling Strategy**

  ------------------------------------------------------------------------
  **Scale Stage** **Tenant        **Database Strategy**
                  Count**         
  --------------- --------------- ----------------------------------------
  Launch (v1.0)   0--500 tenants  Single primary PostgreSQL instance with
                                  1 read replica.

  Growth          500--2,500      Primary + 2 read replicas. Read replicas
  (v1.1--v2.0)    tenants         serve reporting queries and dashboard
                                  loads.

  Scale           2,500--10,000   Horizontal read replica scaling.
                  tenants         Consider table partitioning by tenant_id
                                  for largest tables.

  Enterprise tier High-volume     Option to migrate a single large tenant
                  single tenant   to a dedicated PostgreSQL instance.
  ------------------------------------------------------------------------

**3.6 Prisma + RLS Implementation Pattern**

  -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  **⚠️ Critical Implementation Note: Prisma does not natively support SET
  LOCAL per-transaction tenant context injection. Read this section
  before any data access code is written.**

  -----------------------------------------------------------------------

**3.6.1 The Incompatibility**

Prisma\'s query engine manages a connection pool internally. When you
call prisma.projects.findMany(), Prisma checks out a connection from its
pool, executes the query, and returns the connection. There is no
Prisma-native hook to execute SET LOCAL app.current_tenant_id before the
query on that specific connection. If SET LOCAL is executed on
connection A and Prisma\'s query runs on connection B, RLS sees no
tenant context and returns zero rows or throws an error.

**3.6.2 The Solution --- Dual Client Pattern**

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+
| import { PrismaClient } from \'@prisma/client\';                      |
|                                                                       |
| import { Pool } from \'pg\';                                          |
|                                                                       |
| // Prisma --- used for all standard queries (type-safe, migrations)   |
|                                                                       |
| export const prisma = new PrismaClient();                             |
|                                                                       |
| // Raw pg pool --- used ONLY for tenant context injection             |
|                                                                       |
| export const pgPool = new Pool({                                      |
|                                                                       |
| connectionString: process.env.DATABASE_URL,                           |
|                                                                       |
| max: 10,                                                              |
|                                                                       |
| });                                                                   |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+

**3.6.3 withTenantContext Implementation**

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+
| export async function withTenantContext(tenantId, queryFn) {          |
|                                                                       |
| const client = await pgPool.connect();                                |
|                                                                       |
| try {                                                                 |
|                                                                       |
| await client.query(\'BEGIN\');                                        |
|                                                                       |
| await client.query(\'SET LOCAL app.current_tenant_id = \$1\',         |
| \[tenantId\]);                                                        |
|                                                                       |
| const result = await queryFn(client);                                 |
|                                                                       |
| await client.query(\'COMMIT\');                                       |
|                                                                       |
| return result;                                                        |
|                                                                       |
| } catch (err) {                                                       |
|                                                                       |
| await client.query(\'ROLLBACK\');                                     |
|                                                                       |
| throw err;                                                            |
|                                                                       |
| } finally {                                                           |
|                                                                       |
| client.release();                                                     |
|                                                                       |
| }                                                                     |
|                                                                       |
| }                                                                     |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+

Use client.query() (raw pg) for all tenant-scoped operations inside
withTenantContext. Use Prisma for schema migrations, non-tenant-scoped
lookups, and the Super Admin layer (BYPASSRLS).

**3.6.4 Engineering Checklist**

-   PgBouncer configured in transaction mode --- validated in staging

-   withTenantContext wrapper reviewed and approved by tech lead

-   All tenant-scoped service methods use withTenantContext --- enforced
    in code review

-   Linting rule added: flag any direct prisma.\* call on tenant-scoped
    tables outside withTenantContext

-   Integration test: verify query run without tenant context returns
    zero rows (not an error)

**3.7 withTenantContext Exception Safety**

**3.7.1 Why SET LOCAL Is the Critical Choice**

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+
| SET app.current_tenant_id = \'t_001\'; \-- Session-scoped: persists   |
| for connection lifetime                                               |
|                                                                       |
| SET LOCAL app.current_tenant_id = \'t_001\'; \-- Transaction-scoped:  |
| cleared on COMMIT or ROLLBACK                                         |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+

The architecture uses SET LOCAL. This is not a stylistic choice --- it
is the safety mechanism. SET LOCAL variables are scoped to the
transaction and automatically cleared on COMMIT or ROLLBACK. When
PgBouncer kills a connection mid-transaction, the incomplete transaction
is rolled back by PostgreSQL, clearing all SET LOCAL variables.
PgBouncer discards the killed connection rather than returning it to the
pool, replacing it with a fresh connection that has no stale state.

**3.7.2 PgBouncer server_reset_query --- Backstop Configuration**

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+
| \# pgbouncer.ini                                                      |
|                                                                       |
| \[pgbouncer\]                                                         |
|                                                                       |
| pool_mode = transaction \# CRITICAL --- must be transaction, not      |
| session                                                               |
|                                                                       |
| server_reset_query = DISCARD ALL \# Defense-in-depth: clears all      |
| session state on connection return                                    |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+

**3.7.3 What Would Make This Unsafe**

  ------------------------------------------------------------------------
  **Condition**         **Risk**                 **Mitigation**
  --------------------- ------------------------ -------------------------
  SET SESSION used      Session-scoped context   Always use SET LOCAL ---
  instead of SET LOCAL  survives transaction end enforced in code review
                        --- could leak to next   (T-48)
                        tenant                   

  PgBouncer in session  Connections returned to  PgBouncer must be in
  mode                  pool after transaction   transaction mode (T-43)
                        --- session variables    
                        persist                  

  server_reset_query    PgBouncer does not reset Configure
  not configured        connection state between server_reset_query =
                        uses                     DISCARD ALL (T-49)
  ------------------------------------------------------------------------

